From 93a8b6ee01313da2cd0da836916235071cd1f9c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: basalt <basalt@localhost>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 07:20:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ram-guard: --ram-guard-mib N (0 = the RAM guard off)

The serve RAM guard's floor is the automatic margin plus one prompt chunk's file
reads. --ram-guard-mib N replaces it; 0 turns the guard off: nothing is shed and
no request stops for the guard, so the OS reclaims its page cache and swaps
instead. Placement, the tiers and the chunk sizes are untouched.

Base: 64a935db
---
 docs/configuration/index.md        |  1 +
 docs/running.md                    |  7 ++++++-
 include/basalt/platform/memory.hpp |  3 ++-
 include/basalt/program/options.hpp |  5 +++++
 src/platform/memory.cpp            |  2 +-
 src/program/cli/arguments.cpp      | 10 ++++++++++
 src/program/cli/help.cpp           |  6 +++++-
 src/program/serve/memory.cpp       |  5 ++++-
 tests/platform/memory_test.cpp     |  2 ++
 9 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/configuration/index.md b/docs/configuration/index.md
index 88cc91f9..a7078097 100644
--- a/docs/configuration/index.md
+++ b/docs/configuration/index.md
@@ -148,6 +148,7 @@ The model's experts live in VRAM (the expert cache), in RAM, or in the file. The
 | `vram-reserve-mib` | engine | integer MiB, at least 256 (128 on Linux) | 700 (lowered automatically, down to 300, on a card that would otherwise get too few slots; a value you give is kept unless it is raised as described here) | VRAM left free on the main card for graphs, scratch and the head when `expert-cache auto` sizes the cache. A server's kernels and window graphs that load after the cache (96 MiB, plus 24 MiB per slot with `--parallel`) load into it; a reserve too small to hold them and keep the floor free is raised to that: on Linux with `--parallel 4`, any value below 320 MiB (128 + 96 + 4 x 24) becomes 320, and the `expert cache auto` start line gives both (`320 MiB reserved (150 asked, ...)`). Below the floor is refused. Raise it if the machine pages GPU memory or other programs need VRAM. |
 | `resident-experts` | engine | switch | on when the engine has an expert profile for the model | Keep the experts the GPU does not hold in page-locked RAM, so none is read from the file again. Leaves a RAM floor free for the OS; when the RAM cannot hold them it falls back to a plain file mapping. `mmap-experts` turns it off. |
 | `resident-budget-gib` | engine | number GiB | auto when not set | Like `resident-experts` but with at most N GiB of experts in RAM, hottest first. Refused when it would leave less than the RAM floor free; the error names the largest safe value. |
+| `ram-guard-mib` | engine | integer MiB, 0 or more | the RAM margin plus one prompt chunk's file reads | The MemAvailable a serving request must stay above; below it the request stops with an error instead of the machine swapping. `0` turns the guard off: requests run however far the RAM goes and the OS reclaims and swaps. Placement and the tiers are untouched, so output does not change. |
 | `mmap-experts` | engine | switch | off | Opt out of the default resident RAM copy and back to a file mapping. Speed then depends on the OS page cache. |
 | `shared-expert-arena` | engine | file path | off | Back the resident arena with one shared file so several processes use one copy. Put it on `/dev/shm`. |
 | `expert-profile-save` | engine | file path | off | Save what the adaptive tier learned (experts in VRAM and routing counts) to this file on exit and periodically. A start that finds the file places it instead of the engine's profile, so the first request runs on what the last run learned: faster when the workload continues, slower when it changes ([adaptive tier](adaptive.md)). Needs the adaptive tier on. `tools/convert/convert.py --profile` reads it too. |
diff --git a/docs/running.md b/docs/running.md
index 73480368..44acd0e2 100644
--- a/docs/running.md
+++ b/docs/running.md
@@ -226,11 +226,16 @@ Basalt refuses settings that would leave the machine without memory, and never s
   under 1 GiB free, or below the guard while the kernel's memory pressure (`/proc/pressure/memory`, avg10) shows tasks
   stalled most of the time (some >= 60% or full >= 40%). Without a pressure reading (Windows, or a kernel without PSI)
   below the guard is critical. The error (`RAM low (... free < ... guard) ...: request stopped` or `RAM critical (...
-  free < ... hard floor): request stopped`, also returned to the client) names `--resident-budget-gib` only when the
+  free < ... hard floor): request stopped`, also returned to the client) names `--resident-budget-gib` and
+  `--ram-guard-mib` only when the
   engine's own growth took the RAM, otherwise how much other programs took. While the model sleeps the guard judges no
   reading (the driver holds the GPU state in RAM until the wake); the wake takes a fresh one. Windows does not count the
   pages read through the mapped expert file as available, so within 1 GiB of the guard the engine gives them back first
   and reads again.
+- `--ram-guard-mib N` replaces that guard's floor with N MiB, `0` turning the guard off: nothing is shed and no request
+  stops for the guard. Placement, the tiers and the chunk sizes do not change: with the guard off a request runs
+  however far the RAM goes, so the OS reclaims its page cache and swaps and the request slows instead of stopping
+  with an error.
 
 ## Vision: `--vision`
 
diff --git a/include/basalt/platform/memory.hpp b/include/basalt/platform/memory.hpp
index 07fc599d..f74cc5a8 100644
--- a/include/basalt/platform/memory.hpp
+++ b/include/basalt/platform/memory.hpp
@@ -146,9 +146,10 @@ inline bool ram_below_floor(uint64_t avail, uint64_t floor) { return avail > 0 &
 /// give-back (0 = none yet) and `since_ms` the time since it.  Within `lead` of `floor`, the first give-back is due at
 /// once; later ones need the cooldown over and the reading down by `refill` since `after`, i.e. the view has been
 /// read in again.  A reading that stays low with nothing refilling therefore causes no repeated give-backs.
+/// A `floor` of 0 is the guard off (`--ram-guard-mib 0`): nothing is ever given back.
 inline bool reclaim_due(uint64_t avail, uint64_t floor, uint64_t lead, uint64_t after, uint64_t since_ms,
                         uint64_t refill = 256ull << 20, uint64_t cooldown_ms = 250) {
-    if (!ram_below_floor(avail, floor + lead)) return false;
+    if (floor == 0 || !ram_below_floor(avail, floor + lead)) return false;
     if (after == 0) return true;
     return since_ms >= cooldown_ms && avail + refill <= after;
 }
diff --git a/include/basalt/program/options.hpp b/include/basalt/program/options.hpp
index 8886548b..b2ba5303 100644
--- a/include/basalt/program/options.hpp
+++ b/include/basalt/program/options.hpp
@@ -118,6 +118,11 @@ struct Options {
     /// this reserve for the graphs, the hit scratch and the head.
     int vram_reserve_mib = 700;
     bool vram_reserve_given = false;   // --vram-reserve-mib on the command line (no smaller automatic reserve)
+    /// `--ram-guard-mib N`: the free RAM (MemAvailable) a serving request must stay above; below it a request stops
+    /// with an error instead of the machine swapping.  -1 = the automatic floor (the RAM margin plus one prompt
+    /// chunk's file reads, `ram_guard_floor`); 0 = the guard off: requests run however far the RAM goes and the OS
+    /// reclaims its cache and swaps instead.  Placement and the tiers stay as they are; the RAM-home reserve follows.
+    int64_t ram_guard_mib = -1;
     /// Opt-in: the expert cache in physical segments (CUDA virtual memory management), so the serve loop's
     /// `VRAM <reserve_mib>` command can give part of it back to other programs and take it again.  Off: one cudaMalloc.
     bool vram_elastic = false;
diff --git a/src/platform/memory.cpp b/src/platform/memory.cpp
index 674e193a..238b8f8a 100644
--- a/src/platform/memory.cpp
+++ b/src/platform/memory.cpp
@@ -301,7 +301,7 @@ std::string ram_stop_message(uint64_t avail, uint64_t floor, uint64_t hard, cons
                       (unsigned long long) (floor >> 20));
     char tail[160] = "";
     if (own > others)
-        std::snprintf(tail, sizeof tail, "; lower --resident-budget-gib");
+        std::snprintf(tail, sizeof tail, "; lower --resident-budget-gib or set --ram-guard-mib");
     else if (others >= (1ull << 20))
         std::snprintf(tail, sizeof tail, "; other programs took %llu MiB since the engine was ready",
                       (unsigned long long) (others >> 20));
diff --git a/src/program/cli/arguments.cpp b/src/program/cli/arguments.cpp
index 69ec81f2..fce5dbae 100644
--- a/src/program/cli/arguments.cpp
+++ b/src/program/cli/arguments.cpp
@@ -92,6 +92,16 @@ std::optional<int> parse_options(int argc, char** argv, Options& o, std::string&
         else if (a == "--gpu") o.gpu = next("--gpu");
         else if (a == "--gpu-plan") o.gpu_plan = true;
         else if (a == "--vram-reserve-mib") { o.vram_reserve_mib = std::atoi(next("--vram-reserve-mib")); o.vram_reserve_given = true; }
+        else if (a == "--ram-guard-mib") {
+            const char* v = next("--ram-guard-mib");
+            char* end = nullptr;
+            const long mib = std::strtol(v, &end, 10);
+            if (mib < 0 || end == v || *end != '\0') {
+                basalt::log::error("basalt generate", "--ram-guard-mib needs N >= 0 MiB (0 = the guard off)");
+                return 2;
+            }
+            o.ram_guard_mib = mib;
+        }
         else if (a == "--prefill") {
             const std::string v = next("--prefill");
             o.prefill_given = true;
diff --git a/src/program/cli/help.cpp b/src/program/cli/help.cpp
index 8edde7a3..065da802 100644
--- a/src/program/cli/help.cpp
+++ b/src/program/cli/help.cpp
@@ -199,7 +199,11 @@ void usage() {
                  "  --resident-budget-gib N  the resident mode with at most N GiB of experts in RAM (hottest first;\n"
                  "                       auto when not set: what fits);\n"
                  "                       a budget that would leave less than the RAM floor free is refused, naming\n"
-                 "                       the largest safe value.\n");
+                 "                       the largest safe value.\n"
+                 "  --ram-guard-mib N  the RAM (MemAvailable) a serving request must stay above: below it the request\n"
+                 "                       stops with an error instead of the machine swapping.  Auto when not set: the\n"
+                 "                       RAM margin (the larger of 2 GiB and 5% of the RAM) plus one prompt chunk's\n"
+                 "                       file reads; 0 = the guard off (the OS reclaims and swaps instead).\n");
 }
 
 }  // namespace basalt::program::cli
diff --git a/src/program/serve/memory.cpp b/src/program/serve/memory.cpp
index f364f969..b2f9d691 100644
--- a/src/program/serve/memory.cpp
+++ b/src/program/serve/memory.cpp
@@ -284,7 +284,10 @@ int Runtime::init_memory() {
     basalt::platform::stop_memory_watchdog();
     // from here the RAM guard watches: below its floor the engine sheds and new requests wait, and a running
     // request stops only once the RAM is critical
-    guard_floor = basalt::platform::ram_guard_floor(basalt::platform::ram_margin(), tier_file_bytes, prompt_file_bytes);
+    // (--ram-guard-mib overrides that floor; 0 turns the guard off, the placement and the tiers stay as sized)
+    guard_floor = o.ram_guard_mib >= 0
+                      ? (uint64_t) o.ram_guard_mib << 20
+                      : basalt::platform::ram_guard_floor(basalt::platform::ram_margin(), tier_file_bytes, prompt_file_bytes);
     basalt::platform::start_ram_guard(guard_floor);
     disk_floor = guard_floor + (1ull << 30);   // a disk-tier capture or read stays a GiB above the guard
     // what a stop's error compares against, to tell the engine's own growth from other programs'
diff --git a/tests/platform/memory_test.cpp b/tests/platform/memory_test.cpp
index ae1a57da..bd7e2b40 100644
--- a/tests/platform/memory_test.cpp
+++ b/tests/platform/memory_test.cpp
@@ -96,6 +96,7 @@ int ram_floor_cases() {
     check("serve guard, experts read from the file: margin + 3.5", guard / G, 5.5);
     check("serve guard, only part of a chunk read from the file", ram_guard_floor(margin, all_q4, gib(1)) / G, 3.0);
     check("serve guard, nothing read from the file: the margin", ram_guard_floor(margin, 0, prompt) / G, 2.0);
+    check("serve guard --ram-guard-mib 0: a low reading still runs", ram_below_floor(gib(0.1), 0) ? 1.0 : 0.0, 0.0);
     struct Case { const char* what; double avail; bool low; };
     const Case cases[] = {
         {"well above the floor", 20.0, false},
@@ -393,6 +394,7 @@ int reclaim_due_cases() {
     check("less than a chunk refilled: no", reclaim_due(after - step + 1, floor, lead, after, 10000), false);
     check("low and nothing refilling: no", reclaim_due(floor + lead - 1, floor, lead, floor + lead - 1, 10000), false);
     check("rose above the lead, fell back a chunk: yes", reclaim_due(floor + lead - 1, floor, lead, gib(9.0), 250), true);
+    check("the guard off (floor 0): no", reclaim_due(gib(0.5), 0, lead, 0, 0), false);
     return fail;
 }
 // The watchdog's line: the budget flag is named only when the resident copy is big enough for it to shrink.
